  ],
  "text": "At the peak, one tulip bulb traded for the price of a canal house in Amsterdam. A bulb is a plant. It does nothing. Nobody was arguing about what it was for. But new, rare, and impossible to make more of quickly. That turns out to be enough. Buyers stopped buying flowers and started buying contracts for flowers. Then one morning in sixteen thirty seven, an auction found no bidders. Nothing had changed about tulips. Only what people believed about tomorrow. And the shape repeats. South Sea, nineteen twenty nine, dot com, housing in two thousand eight. New asset, easy credit, a story about endless growth, then a sudden loss of nerve. Be careful with the legend though. Historians now think tulip mania was smaller than the story says. The pattern is completely real. The tulip is just the version that tells best. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
